WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
This is the airport on Papa Westray,
 one of the smallest of the Orkney Islands,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.680
off the tip of Scotland. 

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.560
It is just a windsock, and a hut,
 and a gravel runway.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.560
I didn't pick a great day
 to travel to this tiny little island.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.440
The rain is going to return in just
a few minutes, but it's still been a

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.680
nice little trip out to visit the world's
 oldest still standing house.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.760
[wind and rain noise]

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.320
It's raining sideways. 

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.760
...and also watch some seabirds. 

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.960
From quite a distance. 

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.320
That's a bird.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.960
...that's a bird.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.240
That is also a bird.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.640
But leaving here, I'm taking the world's
 shortest commercial flight

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.240
from this airport to the
next island, a mile away.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.480
Which sounded like a great video. 

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.720
But as I started to write the script,
 I found some problems. 

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.640
It's a commercial flight
 and it's August 2021 as I record this.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.440
So masks are required,
 which won't look great.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.520
And the planes in use on this route
are incredibly loud and rattly.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:55.640
You wouldn't hear a thing. 

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.840
And I'm not going to be the jackass
 who's vlogging on a tiny little plane

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.640
with other passengers on it, loudly
 talking and bothering people and

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.080
accidentally poking a selfie
 stick in someone's face. 

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.520
I'm just going to swap this GoPro out
for a 360-camera,

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.200
and just record voiceover afterwards.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.280
But... a video about commercial aviation
 logistics, using voiceover?

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.000
Well, that doesn't sound like me. 

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.760
That sounds like something that the
Wendover Productions channel would do.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.360
So, Sam?

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.000
- Hey there, Tom!
 - I'm going to leave this up to you.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:24.880
I'm going to go catch my flight.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.200
Spanning a distance of  1.7 miles or 2.7 km
over some 80-or-so seconds,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.920
the flight Tom's about to take

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.880
might seem an affront to logic, economics,
environmentalism and more.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.320
But the world's shortest flight has
 a very real purpose for very real people.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.480
You see, the Orkney Islands are a
sparsely-populated archipelago.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.480
The vast majority of their 22,000-strong
 population lives on what's known,

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.920
despite its island status,
 as the "mainland",

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.360
leaving only about 4,000
 on the outer islands.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.640
These outer islanders need ways
 of getting to the mainland, of course,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.240
but bridges are expensive, 

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.360
especially over some of the longer
 interisland spans in the Orkneys.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.080
And while ferries do operate
 throughout the archipelago,

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.880
they're slow and lack a direct connection
 to onward travel.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.240
Therefore, the answer is airplanes.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.240
Of course, flying is expensive. 

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.320
And in the world of commercial aviation,
 it's typically the case

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.880
that the lower the demand,
 the more expensive it is

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.160
for an airline to operate a route
 on a per passenger basis.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.440
That's because smaller destinations lack
 the economies of scale afforded

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.080
by operating larger airplanes
 more frequently at larger airports.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.920
So air service to places with 600 or 90
 residents like Westray and Papa Westray

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.840
is essentially impossible
 on a free market basis.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.800
The fares would be enormous per passenger,
 meaning people wouldn't choose to fly.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.520
Meaning there wouldn't be enough
 passengers to fill a plane.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.160
Therefore, flights to Westray and Papa
 Westray are subsidized through the UK's

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.280
public service obligation scheme,
 meaning only a small portion of Loganir's

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.320
revenue is earned through
 selling the flight's £17 or £18 tickets.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.520
Still, filling a nine-passenger plane
 to Papa Westray, in particular,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:07.840
with its 90-person population would be
 difficult even at rock bottom prices.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.240
Price elasticity only goes so far.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:16.080
Therefore, Loganair uses a network design
 referred to as a "milk run".

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.640
Essentially, it means operating a plane
 like a bus or train.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.120
In this case, the BN-2 Islander aircraft
leaves Kirkwall, the largest town

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.840
in the Orkneys, with passengers
 destined for Papa Westray and Westray.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.640
Then it lands in Papa Westray,
 drops off any passengers destined for

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.640
that island and picks up any passengers
 traveling to Westray and Kirkwall.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.840
Next, it flies those 80 or so
seconds to Westray,

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.720
drops off its passengers and picks up
any final travelers headed for Kirkwall.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:48.200
This way, it aggregates the cumulative
 demand of two islands onto one flight,

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:53.000
allowing for operations to each more regularly
than if they both had dedicated flights.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.480
Altogether, this means the only people
 that might take the flight exclusively

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.520
between Papa Westray and Westray
 are aviation enthusiasts and tourists

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:04.480
who make the pilgrimage to fly
 on the world's shortest commercial flight.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.720
Tom, however, is returning to Kirkwall.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.280
- Sam, thank you so much. 

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.280
There's a link to Sam's channel,
 Wendover Productions, 

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.680
in the description and on screen.
And sure enough, 

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.840
the plane spent two minutes on the ground
 while one passenger disembarked

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.280
and the pilot filled out some paperwork,
 and then we were off in the air again.

